          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why do some categories need a fourth or fifth character while others do not?",
          "answer": "Some categories in the ICD-10 coding system require a fourth or fifth character because they need more specificity to describe the condition accurately. Most ICD-10-CM categories use the third character to denote the primary category, while additional characters add details like the cause, anatomical site, or severity. For example, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (J44) includes a fourth character to indicate complications present during the encounter (source: \"ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection\").\n\nHowever, certain codes, such as I10 for essential hypertension, do not require additional characters to convey their full meaning. These codes are considered complete with only three characters, which is less common in the ICD-10-CM system (source: \"Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family\").\n\nI could not cover specific examples that illustrate when and why certain conditions require additional characters beyond the general explanation.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
